  "question": {
    "text": "Three blocks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> are lying on a smooth horizontal surface, as shown in the figure. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> have equal masses, <math><mi>m</mi></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> has mass <math><mi>M</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is given an initial speed <math><mi>v</mi></math> towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> due to which it collides with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> perfectly inelastically. The combined mass collides with <math><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo></math> also perfectly inelastically .&nbsp;<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mtext>t</mtext><mtext>h</mtext></math> of the initial kinetic energy is lost in the whole process. What is the value of <math><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></math>?<br /><br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0093_979f030c04a0.png\" style=\"height: 46px; width: 200px;\" />"
  },
